#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134644} :activity/id #uuid "b494fad7-04fa-5835-9125-27e96987941d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049951 :topic/id #uuid "20a638bb-c3f9-5b18-a306-728a49ef4638" :topic/math-academy-id 2099} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135037 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ be vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$. What do we mean when we say that the vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are orthogonal?\n\nTwo vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are **orthogonal** if their dot product equals zero: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=0\n$$\n\nIf two vectors are orthogonal, then we denote this by $\\mathbf{u} \\!\\perp\\! \\mathbf{v}$. For instance, the vectors \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nin $\\mathbb{R^2}$ are orthogonal, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is orthogonal to any vector $\\mathbf{u}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ since $\\mathbf{0}\\cdot\\mathbf{u}=0$." :tutorial/id #uuid "d602fe3a-c5cb-5b19-b348-5113b034e9f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3608 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "33af035b-34c4-553f-a53d-8f63666f0e02" :step/math-academy-id 20202 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134719}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133770 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133579 :question/id #uuid "7aec717f-6f52-590d-9cd0-47c34b587e1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5551"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ea335d6-6ee6-514f-acd4-deb39d50cf81" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048774}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272028} {:db/id #edb/r